  "method": "For each UTC calendar day (ts / 86400000, integer division, i.e. floor), the row with the maximum ts (the latest intraday observation collected that day) was selected via a correlated subquery. This approximates a daily 'close' using CryptoPulse's own periodically-collected price snapshots -- it is NOT an exchange-published daily close, and intraday timing of the last observation varies (see notes).",
  "query": "SELECT ts, btc_price FROM btc_data b WHERE ts = (SELECT MAX(ts) FROM btc_data b2 WHERE b2.ts/86400000 = b.ts/86400000) ORDER BY ts",
  "unit": "USD",
  "field": "btc_price",
  "notes": [
    "Raw table has 2221 rows spanning 2026-05-05 to 2026-09-27 (~145 days), collected at irregular sub-daily intervals (observed gaps range from ~15 minutes to several hours across the history).",
    "A handful of exact-duplicate (ts, price) pairs exist in the raw table (e.g. ts=1785877205133 appears twice with identical price) -- harmless for daily resampling, deduplicated by the loader.",
    "Earlier in the series (before ~1785799611191 / 2026-06-01), the 'last observation of the UTC day' consistently lands near 00:00:00 UTC (ts is an exact multiple of 86400000), suggesting a period where only one observation per day was retained/collected; later timestamps land at arbitrary intraday times, reflecting more frequent collection. This is disclosed as a data-collection artifact, not smoothed over.",
    "This file is a static point-in-time export for reproducibility. It is NOT re-fetched automatically and will not reflect data added after the extraction time above."
